    'val.vlanid_dup': 'VLAN ID {id} used for "{a}" and "{b}" in the same domain.',
    'val.vlanid_dup_res': 'Use a distinct VLAN ID per network (only the AZ1/AZ2 pair of one network may share an ID).',
    'help.fleet.title': 'VCF Management network models',
    'help.fleet.body': 'VCF 9.1 documents 4 models for fleet components: <ul><li><strong>Shared</strong>: everything on the Management VM Network (default)</li><li><strong>Dedicated VLAN</strong>: one physical VLAN for VCF Management Services, VCF Operations, Automation, License Server</li><li><strong>Dedicated VLAN + NSX Overlay Segment</strong>: Day-0 on the dedicated VLAN, Operations / Automation / Networks move Day-2 to an overlay segment (NSX Edge required)</li><li><strong>Stretched NSX Overlay Segment</strong>: multi-site (Federation) variant of the previous one</li></ul><a href="https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-1/design/design-library/fleet-level-components-networking-detailed-design/fleet-level-components-on-shared-vm-management-network-model.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TechDocs VCF 9.1</a>',
    'help.ms.title': 'VCF Management Services ranges',
    'help.ms.body': 'VCF services runtime nodes take their IPs from a reserved range: <strong>12 IPs minimum, 30 recommended</strong>, plus 6 Log Management IPs (+2 per replica) and 6 Real-time Metrics IPs when deployed. The FQDNs (Fleet, Instance, Services Runtime, Identity Broker, Log Management, VCF Automation) each have an IP <strong>outside</strong> these ranges. VCF Automation has its own 5-IP range. All ranges live on the VCF management network. <a href="https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-1/planning-and-preparation/vcf-components-fqdns-and-ip-addresses/first-vcf-instance-fqdns-and-ip-addresses.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TechDocs VCF 9.1</a>',
    'help.witness.title': 'vSAN Witness',
    'help.witness.body': 'A vSAN Stretched Cluster Witness sits on a third site, reachable over independent L3 from each AZ. By default vmk0 carries management and witness traffic (1 IP); a dedicated vmk1 adds one IP. Witness latency: &lt; 200 ms RTT up to 10 hosts/site, &lt; 100 ms for 11-15. A vMSC (non-vSAN storage) has no vSAN Witness. <a href="https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-1/design/design-library/storage-models-9-x/standard-vsan-storage-model/stretched-cluster-storage-models/vsan-stretched-cluster-network-requirements.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TechDocs VCF 9.1</a>',
    'help.az.title': 'AZ1 / AZ2 networks',
    'help.az.body': 'Broadcom 9.1 table: VM Management is L2 stretched (same VLAN ID); Host Management, vMotion, vSAN and NSX Host Overlay have one network per AZ (same VLAN ID allowed, distinct subnet), routed between AZs. All use a highly available L3 gateway. Ticking "L2 stretched" merges a network into a single row sized for AZ1+AZ2. In vMSC everything is stretched (KB 417356). <a href="https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-1/design/design-library/storage-models-9-x/standard-vsan-storage-model/stretched-cluster-storage-models/vsan-stretched-cluster-network-requirements.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TechDocs VCF 9.1</a>',
    'help.tep.title': 'NSX Host TEP',
    'help.tep.body': 'Each host carries TEP (Geneve) interfaces on the NSX Host Overlay VLAN: 2 per host recommended (active-active). IPs come from an NSX IP pool covering the VLAN range (hosts × TEPs per host). MTU 1600 minimum, 9000 recommended.',
    'help.vpc.title': 'VPC Gateway Connectivity',
    'help.vpc.body': 'VCF Installer 9.1 asks for the Transit Gateway type: <ul><li><strong>Distributed connectivity</strong>: external VLAN + gateway CIDR for the Distributed Transit Gateway, entered at bring-up</li><li><strong>Centralized connectivity</strong>: configured after bring-up by a secondary workflow, on NSX Edges — the workbook\'s pre-filled value</li></ul>Source: VCF 9.1 Planning &amp; Preparation Workbook — Deploy Management Domain → VPC Gateway Connectivity.',
    'help.ipmap.title': 'Per-subnet IP map',
    'help.ipmap.body': 'The 🗺 button of a VLAN row (CIDR filled) shows every address: free, assigned (appliance, VIP, host — hover for the name), gateway, network/broadcast, VLAN range, VCF Management Services / Automation reserved ranges, and <strong>conflicts</strong> (duplicate IP or inside a reserved range). Limited to subnets up to /22.',
    'help.hosts.title': 'ESXi Hosts',
    'help.hosts.body': 'List generated from each domain\'s host count (and AZ1/AZ2 split). Enter each host\'s management IP (vmk0) and FQDN; type only the host number when the CIDR is filled. Other VMkernel IPs come from the VLAN ranges (network pools).',
    // AZ1 / AZ2 network layout (stretched clusters)
